│ patch_description        │ This design replaces the state-space Kalman recursion with a causal, multi-scale robust   │
│                          │ endpoint-regression filter. It draws on the prior programs’ useful low-lag                │
│                          │ principle—emphasizing the newest observations—but avoids a single adaptive tracker whose  │
│                          │ innovation response can create excessive slope reversals.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ For each completed window, the algorithm fits two robust local linear models: a           │
│                          │ short-horizon model for responsiveness and a longer-horizon model for noise suppression.  │
│                          │ Their endpoint estimates are fused according to slope agreement: when both horizons       │
│                          │ indicate the same direction, the short model receives more weight; when they disagree,    │
│                          │ the longer model stabilizes the estimate. A MAD/Huber reweighting pass limits impulses    │
│                          │ and outliers. Finally, an adaptive reversal deadband rejects small direction changes that │
│                          │ are below the locally estimated residual noise level, reducing false reversals without    │
│                          │ introducing moving-average phase delay. Outputs remain causally aligned with the newest   │
│                          │ sample in each window and retain the original public API and output lengths.              │
