│ patch_name               │ multiscale_robust_tracker                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The prior approach used a single exponentially weighted local-linear fit (`0.42 *         │
│                          │ window_size` decay), one Huber pass (`2.35 * scale`), alpha range `0.09–0.64`, and        │
│                          │ two-sample weak-reversal confirmation. Its main weakness was that a single local scale    │
│                          │ could either react to noisy endpoint motion or lag genuine curvature.                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new configuration combines two robust endpoint regressions: a responsive short-scale  │
│                          │ fit (`0.26 * window_size`) and a stable long-scale fit (`0.62 * window_size`). Their      │
│                          │ blend is adaptive: coherent motion favors the short fit, while disagreement or high       │
│                          │ residual noise favors the stable fit. The newest three samples receive asymmetric Huber   │
│                          │ clipping (`1.70 * scale` versus standard `2.45 * scale`) only when they contradict both   │
│                          │ established velocity and fitted slope. The tracker uses a bounded adaptive gain           │
│                          │ (`0.10–0.68`), stronger velocity persistence (`0.84`), noise-relative deadband, and       │
│                          │ three-sample confirmation for weak reversals. Strong, coherent turns bypass confirmation, │
│                          │ reducing lag while suppressing isolated false directional changes.                        │
