│ patch_name               │ robust_multiscale_tracker                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses one exponentially weighted endpoint fit with fixed decay        │
│                          │ (2.85), alpha range 0.18–0.68, beta range 0.012–0.097, and a single innovation-based      │
│                          │ reversal check. This can still allow unstable local slopes and endpoint spikes to         │
│                          │ generate frequent directional changes.                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new configuration separates observation, state tracking, and reversal confirmation.   │
│                          │ It uses: (1) robust full-window and recent-window endpoint regressions, (2) one Huber     │
│                          │ residual-reweighting pass, (3) additional newest-sample contradiction downweighting when  │
│                          │ an endpoint spike opposes both fitted slope and confirmed direction, (4)                  │
│                          │ confidence-weighted blending of long and short horizon measurements, and (5) a two-sample │
│                          │ reversal confirmation gate with a large-event bypass. Lower baseline gains improve        │
│                          │ smoothness and false-reversal suppression, while coherent multi-scale slope agreement and │
│                          │ high-curvature conditions increase gains to preserve responsiveness at genuine turns.     │
